WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:22.115
Hello! Welcome back to Superstars in History, the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with people from the past.

2
00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:24.457
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:25.378 --> 00:00:28.780
Today's guest is one of history's greatest composers.

4
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:36.727
He not only had a remarkable gift for creating complex, emotional music but also kept using that gift

5
00:00:37.108 --> 00:00:38.849
even while growing deaf.

6
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:44.515
Please welcome a true musical genius—Ludwig van Beethoven!

7
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.563
Pardon me, but will this interview take very long?

8
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:06.584
I'm quite a busy man, you know!

9
00:01:06.604 --> 00:01:11.849
I'll er do my best to keep things moving along!

10
00:01:12.929 --> 00:01:15.532
Why don't we start with your famous sketchbooks.

11
00:01:15.952 --> 00:01:18.174
Did you— No, no, no, no!

12
00:01:19.855 --> 00:01:25.960
Let's start with my childhood—that's when my musical gifts first appeared, of course!

13
00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:28.482
No problem, Ludwig.

14
00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:30.704
Let's start wherever you like!

15
00:01:31.552 --> 00:01:32.052
Thank you.

16
00:01:32.713 --> 00:01:36.714
I was born in Bonn, Germany, in 1770.

17
00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:43.217
Both my grandfather and father were singers at the court of the archbishop-elector.

18
00:01:44.017 --> 00:01:48.619
My father taught me how to play the violin and clavier, or keyboard.

19
00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:53.601
When I was about 10 or 11, I left school to focus on music.

20
00:01:54.542 --> 00:02:02.361
My father dreamed of turning me into a child prodigy like Wolfgang Mozart—he hoped to make money from

21
00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:03.241
my talent.

22
00:02:04.181 --> 00:02:07.162
I've heard you had a difficult relationship with your father.

23
00:02:08.243 --> 00:02:12.664
Unfortunately, my father was a very strict and difficult man.

24
00:02:13.244 --> 00:02:18.686
As a child, I loved to make up my own music, but that infuriated him.

25
00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:21.087
"What are you scratching at now?"

26
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.588
he'd yell when I improvised on the violin.

27
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.143
He also drank too much, and at night he'd yank me out of bed and force me to

28
00:02:30.263 --> 00:02:31.664
practice for hours.

29
00:02:32.424 --> 00:02:38.267
I never did become a child prodigy, but I learned by leaps and bounds when I became a

30
00:02:38.347 --> 00:02:41.229
student of Christian Gottlob Neefe.

31
00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:44.090
Who was Christian Gottlob Neefe?

32
00:02:44.571 --> 00:02:45.571
I've never heard of him.

33
00:02:46.672 --> 00:02:54.076
He was the court organist in Bonn—a very important position—and an excellent music teacher.

34
00:02:54.664 --> 00:03:02.369
At about age 12 or so, I became his assistant organist, and Neefe also encouraged my interest in

35
00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:07.473
composing. He even helped me to publish some of my original works!

36
00:03:08.174 --> 00:03:14.358
By the time I was 17, I'd made so much progress that the court gave me money to

37
00:03:14.458 --> 00:03:17.900
travel to Vienna, Europe's center of music.

38
00:03:18.841 --> 00:03:20.362
That's where Mozart lived!

39
00:03:20.822 --> 00:03:22.864
Did you get to meet him while you were there?

40
00:03:23.804 --> 00:03:26.607
That will remain my secret, young lady!

41
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:37.156
I will tell you this—Mozart was heard to say that one day the world would be talking about

42
00:03:37.376 --> 00:03:42.981
me. My time in Vienna had to be cut short when my mother died.

43
00:03:44.062 --> 00:03:46.003
I'm very sorry to hear that.

44
00:03:48.065 --> 00:03:51.188
I stayed in Bonn for the next five years or so.

45
00:03:51.808 --> 00:03:58.550
By now my father couldn't hold a job because of his drinking, so I was my family's primary

46
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:07.112
breadwinner. I continued to work at court, and wealthy parents hired me to teach piano to their children.

47
00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:12.674
That was a job I disliked intensely; I lacked the patience for it!

48
00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:19.476
However, as I met more influential people, more opportunities came my way.

49
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:26.825
I performed at social events and was commissioned to write music for various occasions.

50
00:04:27.545 --> 00:04:34.630
Your life changed forever when the famous composer Joseph Haydn visited Bonn around 1790, right?

51
00:04:36.151 --> 00:04:41.935
Yes, Haydn visited the city and happened to see some of my compositions.

52
00:04:43.356 --> 00:04:48.220
He offered to take me on as his student, and of course, I accepted.

53
00:04:48.864 --> 00:04:55.169
I left Bonn for Vienna in 1792 and never returned.

54
00:04:56.811 --> 00:05:01.414
In Vienna did you focus only on composing music, or did you perform too?

55
00:05:02.375 --> 00:05:09.301
I also performed on the piano, making my first public appearance there in 1795.

56
00:05:09.921 --> 00:05:16.967
During this time I wrote my first and second symphonies, as well as piano sonatas and music for

57
00:05:17.047 --> 00:05:18.188
string quartets.

58
00:05:19.228 --> 00:05:26.974
Most composers were writing music for the harpsichord, but I was different, composing music for the piano.

59
00:05:27.915 --> 00:05:32.358
I was enjoying great success when a personal tragedy struck.

60
00:05:33.419 --> 00:05:35.500
You began losing your hearing?

61
00:05:36.361 --> 00:05:42.645
Yes, to my horror, I was growing deaf, and it haunted me like a ghost!

62
00:05:43.326 --> 00:05:52.075
I was already a rather difficult person; my hearing problem made me more ill-tempered—some might say unbearable!

63
00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:58.398
If you couldn't hear well, Ludwig, how in the world could you keep writing music?

64
00:05:59.299 --> 00:06:01.860
I could still hear music in my head.

65
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.083
I used the sketchbooks you mentioned to write down my ideas, which often came to me while walking

66
00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:09.884
in the country.

67
00:06:10.508 --> 00:06:18.971
Because of my despair over my hearing loss, many of my compositions evoked powerful emotions, full of themes

68
00:06:19.071 --> 00:06:21.351
related to struggles and heroes.

69
00:06:22.292 --> 00:06:25.693
I wrote pieces such as the Piano Sonata no.

70
00:06:25.813 --> 00:06:31.614
14, the Third Symphony, and my one and only opera—Fidelio.

71
00:06:32.615 --> 00:06:34.135
Piano Sonata no.

72
00:06:34.235 --> 00:06:36.976
14 is called the Moonlight Sonata sometimes.

73
00:06:37.772 --> 00:06:41.694
Some people say it was inspired by your love for a woman.

74
00:06:42.115 --> 00:06:42.875
Is that true?

75
00:06:43.115 --> 00:06:47.378
Please, Faye, I'd rather not discuss something so personal!

76
00:06:52.261 --> 00:06:58.184
As for the Third Symphony, that was one of my most important works.

77
00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:06.861
It was longer than any previous symphony, and I dedicated it to Napoleon because I admired the democratic

78
00:07:07.061 --> 00:07:09.022
principles of the French Revolution.

79
00:07:09.823 --> 00:07:19.328
But later—after learning that Napoleon was acting like a king and calling himself emperor—I scrawled out my dedication!

80
00:07:20.409 --> 00:07:21.770
When did you stop performing?

81
00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:32.796
I believe my last public performance was in 1814; after that I turned all my energies to composing.

82
00:07:33.748 --> 00:07:40.953
And during the next part of your life, you composed some of your most amazing works, transforming the

83
00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:43.194
symphony as a musical form.

84
00:07:43.895 --> 00:07:45.276
What can you tell us about this?

85
00:07:46.597 --> 00:07:54.102
Yes, I was quite prolific in my later years, composing piano sonatas as well as my final symphony.

86
00:07:54.902 --> 00:08:02.667
The Ninth Symphony is very long, of course, and it incorporates choral singers, which was unusual and, dare

87
00:08:02.847 --> 00:08:05.602
I say, innovative in its time.

88
00:08:06.342 --> 00:08:11.286
When the symphony was first performed in Vienna, thunderous applause broke out.

89
00:08:12.107 --> 00:08:19.253
Because of my deafness, I didn't realize the audience was clapping until a performer turned me around to

90
00:08:19.393 --> 00:08:20.053
face them.

91
00:08:20.994 --> 00:08:23.976
I must say, the sight impressed even me!

92
00:08:24.817 --> 00:08:28.320
Your compositions have endured for hundreds of years.

93
00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.964
Even today your music is— Faye, I've had quite enough.

94
00:08:33.536 --> 00:08:36.118
May I please return to history now?

95
00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:37.359
Oh yes!

96
00:08:37.739 --> 00:08:39.460
Thank you, Ludwig, and good-bye!

97
00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:48.707
Ludwig van Beethoven died in 1827 at age 57.

98
00:08:49.508 --> 00:08:58.374
Before Beethoven, vocal music was more popular than instrumental; he changed all of that with his compositions, especially

99
00:08:58.474 --> 00:09:00.956
his dazzling symphonies and piano music.

100
00:09:01.748 --> 00:09:07.295
Today his famous Fifth Symphony is among the most frequently played pieces.

101
00:09:09.017 --> 00:09:11.860
I hope you enjoyed meeting Ludwig van Beethoven!

102
00:09:12.581 --> 00:09:16.225
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History.

103
00:09:16.826 --> 00:09:21.771
Don't miss my next interview with another fascinating person from the past!
